{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_100","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"/tmp/apply_patch <<'PATCH'\n--- paper.tex\n+++ paper.tex\n@@ -236,1 +236,1 @@\n-\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_volume_by_condition.pdf}\n+\\\\includegraphics[width=\\\\textwidth]{figures/coverage_volume_by_regime.pdf}\n@@ -175,1 +175,7 @@\n \\\\subsection{Common-Measure Volume and Uncertainty}\n+Development selection used a fixed utility that penalizes a shortfall from nominal coverage and the volume fraction: \"'$U('\"\\\\widehat c,\\\\widehat V)=1-0.8\\\\max(0,0.9-\\\\widehat c)/0.9-0.2\\\\widehat V\"'$. The objective averages across the specified conditions and seeds. It is not a constrained optimization requiring coverage to meet the target: a sufficiently compact region can offset a coverage shortfall in this utility. Conversely, coverage above the target receives no extra reward. The configuration selected by this objective therefore requires a separate scientific assessment of its components. We do not identify selection under that trade-off with satisfaction of every performance target. All observed conditions, including those unfavorable to the frozen configuration, remain in the final comparison.\n+% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L932-L938]]\n+% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L1245-L1263]]\n+\n@@ -359,1 +365,5 @@\n The common volume measure is useful precisely because it is coordinate-independent across the evaluated methods, not because it is the only appropriate utility.\n*** End Patch\nPATCH'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
